# Intent router

Classify the user request before acting. When writing or changing code, apply the Ponytail ladder (`rules/ponytail.mdc`). For structural navigation, prefer MCP `codegraph_explore` (plugin hooks sync the graph on session start, reads, and edits).

## Canonical feature delivery

For new features / multi-step product work, follow this order (also `/feature-pipeline`):

```text
input → brainstorming → grill-me / document / feature list → Spec Kit → implement
```

1. **Input** — request, doc, ticket, or Figma; infer stack; ask once if unclear.
2. **Brainstorming** — `brainstorming` (intent + design approval; hard gate before Spec Kit / product code).
3. **Grill / document / feature list** — `/grill-me` to stress-test; write a short durable brief; produce a scoped feature list (optional `/to-spec`). Wait for confirmation.
4. **Handover to Spec Kit** — feed the brief + feature list into `/speckit-specify` → plan → tasks (constitution/clarify/analyze as needed).
5. **Implement** — optional `prototype` probe, then `/speckit-implement` → `/speckit-converge`.

Skip this pipeline only for pure docs / typo / review / commit / explain, or when the user explicitly skips stages.

## Spec Kit SDD (after handover)

Requires Spec Kit in the target project (`specify init --here --integration cursor-agent`):

1. `/speckit-constitution` — only if constitution missing or still placeholders
2. `/speckit-specify`
3. `/speckit-clarify` — if requirements are ambiguous
4. `/speckit-plan`
5. `/speckit-tasks`
6. `/speckit-analyze` / `/speckit-checklist` — optional quality gates
7. `/speckit-implement` — climb Ponytail ladder while coding; public pages need SEO tags (see `seo-public-pages`); **prototype** when a quick validation probe still helps
8. `/speckit-converge` — repeat with implement until Converged

Do **not** invent a custom Speckit pipeline. Stack: infer from the request + repo.

## Change request → prototype (within implement)

During implement (or for a bounded change after brainstorm/grill), load `prototype` when a throwaway LOGIC/UI probe still answers one open question:

1. State the single question the prototype answers.
2. Choose LOGIC (state/business model) or UI (look/feel variants) per the skill.
3. Build throwaway, trivial-to-run code near the real module/page.
4. Capture the user's verdict, then continue Spec Kit implement / TDD / Ponytail.

**Skip prototype only when** the user explicitly says so, or the ask is pure docs / typo / review / commit / explanation with no behavior or UI change.

## Public page SEO

When building public UI routes, enforce the `seo-public-pages` rule: title, description, canonical, plus robots + sitemap updates.

## Companion and Ponytail skills

| Intent | Skill |
|--------|--------|
| Feature delivery (canonical pipeline) | `/feature-pipeline` |
| Creative work / new feature — intent & design | `brainstorming` |
| Stress-test design before Spec Kit | `grill-me` → `grilling` |
| User document → insight pack / flowchart | `extract-meaning-full-insight` / `document-to-flowchart` |
| Publish feature list / PRD to tracker | `to-spec` |
| Validation probe during implement | `prototype` |
| Correctness / security / maintainability review | `code-review` |
| Over-engineering / delete-list on a diff | `ponytail-review` |
| Whole-repo over-engineering audit | `ponytail-audit` |
| Bug / error / failing test (quick) | `debug` |
| Commit / write message | `commit` |
| Ponytail level / help | `ponytail`, `ponytail-help` |
| New UI / landing / distinctive frontend design | **`hallmark` (required)** + `frontend-design`; MCP `inspo` for real-site refs |
| Figma design → code (Figma MCP / link / node) | `figma-design-to-code` **before** `get_design_context`; then `hallmark` |
| New frontend screen / page | **Mockup first** (same palette) → then implement (`frontend-mockup-first`) |
| Common screen content (Settings, Profile, Auth, …) | `ui-case-studies` |
| Frontend visual update / redesign / UI audit | **`hallmark`** (`audit` / `redesign` / `study` as appropriate) |
| Browser test / screenshot / fill form / QA | `agent-browser` (then `agent-browser skills get core`) |
| React / Next.js write, review, or perf refactor | `vercel-react-best-practices` (+ MCP `next-devtools` if Next.js) |
| Node.js backend / API / middleware / auth / DB service | `nodejs-backend-patterns` |
| NestJS modules / DI / guards / microservices | `nestjs-best-practices` + `nestjs-expert` |
| Deep modules / seams / interface design | `codebase-design` |
| Extract modules → actionable checklist | `extract-modules-checklist` |
| Hard bug (feedback loop / bisection) | `diagnosing-bugs` |
| Test-first / red-green-refactor | `tdd` |
| End-user video demo (HyperFrames) — **optional, user must ask** | `hyperframes-demo` |
| Explicitly delegate impl to Claude Code CLI | `claude-delegate` |

## CodeGraph

Structural questions (call graph, definitions, blast radius, how X reaches Y) → `codegraph_explore` before Read/Grep loops.

## Direct coding

Use for one-file fixes, explanations, small refactors, and questions that do not need a written what/why — **after** brainstorm/grill when the change was creative, and after any agreed prototype gate.

Follow coding standards, constitution, and the Ponytail ladder.
